WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.850
It's amazing how much of an impact someone
we've never actually met can have on our lives.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.469
But that's the magic of movies and television:
sometimes the actors we grow up watching can

00:00:09.469 --> 00:00:12.909
feel just like family, and it feels like they'll
be there forever.

00:00:12.909 --> 00:00:18.330
Sadly, however, we've had to say goodbye to
many talented performers in 2018.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.340
But thanks to the memorable characters they
brought to life, these actors will never really

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.410
leave us.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.820
Here are the unforgettable actors we've already
lost in 2018.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.430
Jerry Van Dyke

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.909
The younger brother of actor Dick Van Dyke
and a four-time Emmy nominee, Jerry Van Dyke

00:00:33.909 --> 00:00:37.559
passed away on January 5th, at the age of
86.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.600
According to TMZ, Van Dyke was in hospice
care before he succumbed to chronic heart

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.600
disease.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.679
One of his earliest TV appearances was on
a 1962 episode of The Dick Van Dyke Show,

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:50.089
playing the brother of his real-life sibling.

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:54.129
He then infamously turned down a starring
role on Gilligan's Island to work on the sitcom

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:57.949
My Mother the Car, which ran for just one
season in 1965.

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:04.540
Then in 1989, he landed the role of Assistant
Coach Luther Van Dam on the ABC's Coach, earning

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.580
him four Emmy nominations for Best Supporting
Actor.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.640
He met up with his brother once again, on
The Middle, in 2015.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:19.500
"We are two of a kind…"

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.180
Reg E. Cathey

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.720
An Emmy-winning actor known for House of Cards
and The Wire, Reg E. Cathey lost his battle

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.580
with lung cancer on February 9th in New York
City.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:33.080
A native of Alabama, Cathey landed small roles
in Born on the Fourth of July, What About

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.850
Bob?, Seven, and American Psycho.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.250
He then went on to star in HBO's Oz, and The
Wire, but he received the most acclaim of

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.420
his career for playing Freddy Hayes on the
Netflix series House of Cards.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.390
The role brought him Emmy nominations for
three straight years, and he won in 2015.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.640
"Hey, in this joint I'm the president, the
vice president, and the congress.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.960
He just another customer."

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.390
John Mahoney

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:02.950
Famous for his turn as Frasier Crane's cranky
dad on Frasier, John Mahoney passed away in

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.640
Chicago on February 4th, at 77.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.050
Mahoney suffered from kidney disease and diabetes,
and died of complications from lung cancer

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.530
and brain disease.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:15.940
Born in England, Mahoney began his acting
career with the Steppenwolf Theater in Chicago

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:21.030
when he was nearly 40 years old, and won a
Tony Award for Best Featured Actor in a Play,

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.810
for 1986's The House of Blue Leaves.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:28.280
Mahoney made his film debut in the early '80s,
but he became a household name when he landed

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.819
the role of Marty Crane on Frasier from 1993
to 2004, receiving two Emmy and two Golden

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:35.819
Globe nominations.

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:40.450
"I'm afraid your magic box will rob me of
my spirit."

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.350
David Ogden Stiers

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:47.819
Remembered as Major Charles Emerson Winchester
the Third on MASH, 75-year-old David Ogden

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:52.800
Stiers lost his battle with bladder cancer
on March 3rd at his home in Newport, Oregon.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.520
Stiers' earliest TV credits include The Mary
Tyler Moore Show, Charlie's Angels and Kojak,

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.300
but he rose to fame when he landed MASH, receiving
two Emmy noms in '81 and '82.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.830
He was also a talented musician, and was the
resident conductor of the Newport Symphony.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:16.000
"The point is you can aspire to a world that
offers you a lot more than comic books."

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.630
DuShon Monique Brown

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:23.580
Best known for her role on Chicago Fire, DuShon
Monique Brown passed away on March 23rd at

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.959
just 49, reportedly of natural causes.

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:32.209
Born in Chicago, Brown rose to fame playing
Nurse Katie Welch on Prison Break from 2005

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:33.380
to 2007.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:39.020
More recently, she portrayed Connie on NBC's
Chicago Fire, co-starring in 53 episodes of

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.910
the series since its debut in 2012.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.800
Donnelly Rhodes

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.160
The Canadian actor best known for playing
Dr. Cottle on Battlestar Galactica, Donnelly

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.770
Rhodes succumbed to cancer on January 8th
at the age of 80.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.210
Rhodes initially became a contract player
for Universal Pictures, landing him on TV

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:01.830
with Mission: Impossible in 1966 and in movies
like Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid in

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:02.830
'69.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:08.880
More recently, he played Agent Smith on The
Flash and DC's Legends of Tomorrow in 2016.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.740
Connie Sawyer

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:16.500
Once known as Hollywood's oldest working actress,
Connie Sawyer passed away on January 22nd.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.160
According to Deadline, she was 105.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.790
Sawyer began acting at just eight years old,
amassing over 140 film and television credits

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:29.000
to her name, and continued acting until late
2017.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.850
In more recent years, Sawyer appeared in When
Harry Met Sally, Dumb and Dumber and Pineapple

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.410
Express, and she guest-starred on Seinfeld,
Will &amp; Grace, and The Office.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.600
"Kid, ya got talent.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.700
Ya look great"

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.060
Robert Dowdell

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:50.500
A supporting actor on the '60s ABC series
Voyage to the Bottom of the Sea, Robert Dowdell

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:53.960
passed away on January 23rd, at 85.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.020
Dowdell appeared frequently on Broadway and
television, but he's perhaps best known for

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.580
playing Chip Morton in Voyage to the Bottom
of the Sea from 1964 to '68.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.720
His final role was for American Masters on
PBS.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.280
Jon Paul Steuer

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.919
The former child actor who played a young
Klingon on Star Trek: The Next Generation,

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:17.670
Jon Paul Steuer passed away on January 1st,
at just 33.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:23.760
Steuer is perhaps best known for appearing
on Grace Under Fire from 1993 through 1996.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.900
His passing was announced on the Facebook
page of his rock band, PROBLEMS.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.530
Emma Chambers

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:34.510
The British actress known for the romantic
comedy Notting Hill, Emma Chambers passed

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:38.370
away on February 21st, at 53, due to natural
causes.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.190
A beloved star of British television, American
audiences probably remember her best as Honey,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:47.440
the eccentric younger sister of Hugh Grant's
character in 1999's Notting Hill.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.530
"Oh you gave me a present.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.440
We're best friends already then."

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.900
John Gavin

00:05:55.389 --> 00:06:01.740
Having played heartthrob Sam Loomis in Psycho,
John Gavin died February 9th, at age 86.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.850
Gavin's career also featured roles in Imitation
of Life, Spartacus, and Fantasy Island.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:12.040
And he was set to play James Bond in 1971's
Diamonds Are Forever before Sean Connery reclaimed

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.120
the role from George Lazenby.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.860
Debbie Lee Carrington

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:20.651
An actress and stuntwoman who appeared in
Total Recall and Men in Black, 58-year-old

00:06:20.651 --> 00:06:25.380
Debbie Lee Carrington passed away on March
23rd at her parents' home in California.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:30.090
Carrington began her career in 1981 with the
comedy Under the Rainbow, based on the Munchkins

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.830
from The Wizard of Oz.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.770
Not long after, she landed the part of an
Ewok in 1983's Return of the Jedi, then played

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:41.389
another Ewok in the 1984 TV movie, The Ewok
Adventure.

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:45.460
Although she often served as a stuntwoman
for child actors or stood in for characters

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:49.810
in films like the Child's Play franchise,
Carrington is perhaps best known for playing

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:52.650
the character, Thumbelina, in 1990's Total
Recall.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.199
She also appeared on Seinfeld, Baywatch and
The Drew Carey Show.

00:06:56.199 --> 00:07:00.180
"Hey you look different, have you been workin
out?

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.180
Not that I know of.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.560
Well whatever it is you're doing, keep doing
it.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:04.560
You look great."

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:05.920
Dorothy Malone

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:11.560
Oscar-winning actress and star of Peyton Place,
Dorothy Malone passed away on January 19th,

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.620
at 92.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:16.320
According to The Hollywood Reporter, she had
been ill for several years.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.410
Malone had appeared in dozens of films through
the mid-'60s when she took the role of Constance

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:26.220
MacKenzie on ABC's Peyton Place, the first
primetime soap opera on U.S. television.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.470
Malone later returned to the franchise for
the 1977 TV movie Murder in Peyton Place.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:35.370
Her final screen performance was in the 1992
thriller Basic Instinct.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.360
"There's only so much a woman can do, and
no more.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:42.530
I speak from personal experience."

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:43.530
Joseph Wayne Miller

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:49.259
A former child actor who broke out with a
role in the 1995 comedy Heavyweights, Joseph

00:07:49.259 --> 00:07:52.850
Wayne Miller died on January 9th at the age
of 36.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:57.180
According to TMZ, Miller's mother said he
passed away in his sleep.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.570
Miller played Salami Sam in Heavyweights,
co-written and produced by Judd Apatow, who

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:03.490
said in a statement to Page Six,

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.790
"Being around Joe Miller was pure joy.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.259
We could not have loved spending time with
him more.

00:08:08.259 --> 00:08:10.449
He made everyone around him so happy.

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:12.449
What a terrible loss."

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:13.740
Vic Damone

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.669
The singer and actor who Frank Sinatra once
said had "the best pipes in the business,"

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:23.950
Vic Damone passed at age 89 on February 11th,
due to complications of a respiratory illness.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:28.919
Damone began his career as winner of Arthur
Godfrey's Talent Scouts in 1947.

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:32.930
His first single, "I Have But One Heart,"
hit Number 7 on the Billboard chart and he

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:37.700
released a string of hits including "You're
Breaking My Heart" in 1949 and "On the Street

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.240
Where You Live" in 1955.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.750
He moved into film acting in the '50s, and
by the '70s, Damone was performing in Las

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:49.800
Vegas casinos when Francis Ford Coppola offered
him the role of Johnny Fontane in The Godfather.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:55.380
Damone reportedly turned down the part because
he was afraid of provoking the mob, and Sinatra.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.100
Harry Anderson

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:01.750
The actor and comedian best known for playing
a young, unorthodox judge on Night Court,

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:07.000
Harry Anderson passed away on April 16th,
at 65, due to natural causes.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:11.510
Anderson began his career as a magician, and
by 1984, he found mainstream fame as Judge

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:16.029
Harry T. Stone on the hit comedy Night Court,
earning three Emmy nominations during the

00:09:16.029 --> 00:09:17.520
show's nine-season run.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.460
While he went on to land additional roles
and multiple guest appearances on TV, Anderson's

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:27.620
final television appearance was an informal
Night Court reunion on 30 Rock in 2008.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:34.750
"Your hope and your chance is where you feel
your heart is."

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:35.750
R. Lee Ermey

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:41.100
A military veteran and Golden Globe-nominated
actor best known for his role as Gunnery Sergeant

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:46.230
Hartman in Full Metal Jacket, R. Lee Ermey
passed away on April 15th, at 74.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:50.380
According to The Hollywood Reporter, Ermey
died from complications of pneumonia.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:55.360
After 11 years of service, Ermey retired from
the military and transitioned into acting.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.380
In 1978, he played a Marine drill instructor
in The Boys in Company C, then landed a role

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:04.350
as a helicopter pilot in 1979's Apocalypse
Now.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:08.660
But it was Stanley Kubrick's Full Metal Jacket
that made Ermey a star, earning him a Golden

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:12.779
Globe nom and a Best Supporting Actor award
from the Boston Society of Film Critics.

00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:18.580
He also played a similar character in Peter
Jackson's 1996 horror-comedy The Frighteners

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:24.260
and appeared in Fletch Lives, Seven, and the
2003 remake of The Texas Chainsaw Massacre.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:29.250
Ermey voiced several TV shows, video games
and movies including SpongeBob SquarePants

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:30.760
and the Toy Story films.

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:31.760
"Hoo rah.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:32.920
Semper Fi."

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.080
Margot Kidder

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:39.410
Margot Kidder, who played DC Comics character
Lois Lane in the Superman film series, died

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:43.769
on May 13, 2018 at her home in Livingston,
Montana.

00:10:43.769 --> 00:10:45.630
She was 69 years old.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.330
Born in Yellowknife, Northwest Territories,
Kidder dreamed of acting from a young age.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:54.700
She landed her first film role in 1969 and
appeared in more than a hundred movie and

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:58.990
television roles over the course of her long
career., including the horror classic The

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:00.589
Amityville Horror.

00:11:00.589 --> 00:11:04.600
But she will always be best known for playing
Lois Lane opposite Christopher Reeve in four

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:08.940
Superman films between 1978 and 1989.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:12.880
Kidder suffered from mental illness all of
her life, and her struggle with bipolar disorder

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.120
at one point led to a high-profile disappearance.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:20.660
She became a mental health advocate, and was
also actively involved in causes she believed

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:24.210
in, protesting against war and the excesses
of the energy industry.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.930
"We're headed down the road to armageddon.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:30.550
And so I've been working a lot with various
peace groups around the country doing whatever

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:31.550
I can."

